From e4bf7cc8d2b9bd4157a786b9e4a285099687c60a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 10:48:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Search String units in place in indexes, split, contains
 and affixes

String.indexes copied both its needle and its haystack into std::u16strings on every call, and
String.split did the same whenever its operands were not both UTF-8, so searching a short ASCII
needle such as "<" or "\"" in a UTF-16 String or slice paid two heap copies and a UTF-16 widening
each time. Html parsers call these millions of times on slices of one page. contains, startsWith
and endsWith took a byte path only when both operands were UTF-8 and fell back to charAt per unit
for a UTF-8 needle against a UTF-16 haystack, the common mixed case.

A UnitRun now reads any form whose units lie contiguously in memory without copying: a UTF-16 leaf,
a large String or a slice as UTF-16 units, a UTF-8 leaf or view as ASCII bytes. forEachUnitMatch
searches one run in another for all four pairings of unit widths, comparing units by value (an ASCII
byte equals its UTF-16 unit): Boyer-Moore-Horspool for needles of 4 or more units as before, and a
first-unit scan with memchr over bytes for shorter ones. indexes keeps up to 16 results on the stack;
split builds its pieces with slice() for every non-rope pairing, as the all-UTF-8 path already did,
so long pieces share the source and short ASCII ones become UTF-8 leaves. Ropes keep the copying
paths.

contains, startsWith and endsWith move out of line into StringOps.cpp. Besides keeping the header
small, a strong definition there replaces the inline copies in objects built from the old header,
which matters to builds that relink a prebuilt runtime with this file rebuilt.

On the Elm OS Hacker News bench (callgrind) String.indexes falls from 10.3% to 5.6% of instructions
and loading a comments page from 293 ms to about 220 ms.
